---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const outlierCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'outlier', label: 'fifth value (the outlier)', min: 8, max: 1000, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'k' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'mean of {2, 4, 6, 8, x}', expr: '(2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + outlier)/5' },
    { label: 'median (frozen at 6)', expr: '6' },
    { label: 'mean − median gap', expr: 'abs((2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + outlier)/5 - 6)' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/foundational/describing-data">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A dataset of a million numbers is unreadable; statistics compresses it into a few honest summaries.
    A <strong>centre</strong> (mean, median or mode), a <strong>spread</strong> (range, interquartile
    range, standard deviation), and a <strong>shape</strong> (the histogram). Three numbers can stand
    in for a million — but only if you choose the right three, because each summary answers a slightly
    different question, and they disagree most exactly when it matters. Choosing well is not bookkeeping;
    it is the difference between describing the data and misdescribing it. This lesson builds the whole
    toolkit: how each measure is computed on small sets, how each is <em>estimated</em> when data arrive
    pre-bucketed into groups, and — the exam skill that transfers to real life — when to use which, with
    reasons you can defend.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four calm values and one loud stranger — drag the outlier and watch which summary flinches" widget="sliderLab" config={outlierCfg} />

  <p>
    The widget above contains the lesson in miniature. The set &#123;2, 4, 6, 8, 10&#125; has mean 6 and median 6 —
    perfect agreement. Drag the fifth value toward 1,000 and the mean chases it (dragging four other
    perfectly reasonable values up to 204 with it), while the median sits frozen at 6. One extreme value
    moves the mean by an amount equal to that value's <em>excess</em> over the others, divided by five; it
    cannot move the median at all unless it crosses the middle. That single asymmetry is why "average
    income" (mean, inflated by a few enormous fortunes) and "median income" (the middle person) tell
    different stories about the same country — and why news reports that swap them quietly are editing
    the story.
  </p>

  <h2>Three measures of centre</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>mean</strong> is the sum divided by the count. For the five data values 2, 4, 6, 8, 10,
    the mean is 30 ÷ 5 = 6. It has two wonderful properties: it uses <em>every</em> value (no information
    discarded), and it balances the data like a seesaw — the total distance of points below the mean
    equals the total distance above it, so the mean is the unique balance point. Its weakness is the
    same fact: every value votes, so every value can be dragged.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>median</strong> is the middle value when the data are ordered. With an odd count, take
    the middle one (&#123;1, 3, <strong>9</strong>, 15, 22&#125; → 9). With an even count, average the two middle
    ones (&#123;1, 3, 9, 15&#125; → (3 + 9) ÷ 2 = 6). The median only cares about <em>rank</em>, which is exactly
    why it is immune to outliers and the default for incomes, house prices and waiting times. Its cost:
    it ignores how far values sit from the middle — a dataset of 5s and one 5,000,000 looks the same to
    the median as one of 5s and one 6, when they are wildly different realities.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>mode</strong> is the most frequent value. It is the only centre that works on categories:
    "the modal blood type is O" or "the modal colour sold is black" are meaningful, while a "mean blood
    type" is nonsense. For data given as an <strong>ungrouped frequency distribution</strong> — a table of
    values with counts, say shoe sizes 6, 7, 8, 9 with frequencies 3, 9, 7, 1 — the mode is simply the
    value with the highest frequency (7, appearing 9 times). The mean of such a table is computed by
    weighting each value by its frequency: (6×3 + 7×9 + 8×7 + 9×1) ÷ 20 = 140 ÷ 20 = 7.0. The median is
    the value of the (n + 1) ÷ 2 = 10.5th ordered item — between the 9th (a 7) and 10th (an 8), so
    median = 7. A dataset can have one mode, two modes (<strong>bimodal</strong> — often a signal of two
    mixed populations, like two peaks in a histogram of commute times from two different towns), or none
    where ties occur.
  </p>

  <h2>Grouped data: estimating from buckets</h2>
  <p>
    Real datasets often arrive <strong>grouped</strong>: not individual values but a frequency table of
    classes, e.g. waiting times 0–10 min (14 customers), 10–20 (22), 20–30 (12), 30–40 (2), n = 50. The
    individual values are gone, so we estimate. The <strong>modal class</strong> is the class with the
    highest frequency — here 10–20. The estimated mean assumes every value in a class sits at its
    <strong>midpoint</strong>: (5×14 + 15×22 + 25×12 + 35×2) ÷ 50 = (70 + 330 + 300 + 70) ÷ 50 = 770 ÷ 50
    = 15.4 minutes. The estimate is honest only if values really cluster near midpoints; heavy
    concentration at a class edge biases it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median (and quartiles and percentiles) of grouped data are found by <strong>interpolation</strong>
    within the class that contains the position you need. The formula: take the position n/2 = 25, run
    down cumulative frequencies (14, then 36) to find the median class 10–20, then median = L +
    ((n/2 − CF) ÷ f) × c, where L is the class's lower boundary, CF the cumulative frequency before it,
    f its frequency, c its width: 10 + ((25 − 14) ÷ 22) × 10 ≈ 15.0 minutes. The logic is simple and worth
    internalising rather than memorising: within that class of 22 people, we walk (25 − 14) = 11 people
    in from the left edge, so we travel 11/22 of the class width. The same recipe with n/4 or n×p gives
    any quartile or percentile. These are estimates — the price of bucketed data — and every exam answer
    should say "estimated".
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the midpoint estimate assumes values are spread evenly within each
  class. If the 10–20 bucket is really everyone sitting at 11 minutes plus a few at 19, the true mean is
  below the estimate. Grouping always costs information; the formulas are the best recovery of it, not a
  recovery of all of it.</p>

  <h2>Spread: range, IQR, standard deviation</h2>
  <p>
    A centre without a spread is a headline without a story. Two classes can share a mean of 70 while one
    contains every student between 68 and 72 and the other spans 40 to 99. The <strong>range</strong>
    (maximum − minimum) is the crudest measure: for &#123;2, 4, 6, 8, 10&#125; it is 8. It is fast but defined by
    exactly the two most extreme points in the dataset — the two most likely to be errors.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>interquartile range</strong> is the range of the middle 50%. The lower quartile Q1 sits a
    quarter of the way up the ordered data, the upper quartile Q3 three quarters up; IQR = Q3 − Q1. For
    &#123;2, 4, 6, 8, 10&#125;: Q1 = 3, Q3 = 9, IQR = 6. (Convention matters — Cambridge takes Q1 at the
    (n + 1)/4th value and Q3 at the 3(n + 1)/4th for small sets; interpolation handles grouped data as
    above.) The IQR ignores the top and bottom quarters entirely, so it is <strong>resistant to
    outliers</strong> and pairs naturally with the median. It also powers the standard outlier rule: a
    value more than 1.5 × IQR beyond Q1 or Q3 earns investigation as a suspected outlier — a rule of
    thumb, not a verdict.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>standard deviation</strong> asks a sharper question: on average, how far do values sit
    from the mean? Its computation reveals the concept: find each deviation (x − mean), square it (so
    negatives cannot cancel positives), average the squares, then square-root to undo the distortion.
    For &#123;2, 4, 6, 8, 10&#125; with mean 6: deviations are −4, −2, 0, 2, 4; squares are 16, 4, 0, 4, 16,
    totalling 40; the <strong>variance</strong> is 40 ÷ 5 = 8 (for a population; a sample divides by
    n − 1, giving 10, to correct a slight under-estimate); the standard deviation is √8 ≈ 2.83. The SD
    uses every value and is the backbone of inference — but because it squares deviations, it is
    <strong>hugely sensitive to outliers</strong>: a single 100 in that dataset would blow the SD up by
    a factor of many while barely moving the median or IQR. For bell-shaped data, the
    <strong>68–95–99.7 rule</strong> makes the SD interpretable: about 68% of values fall within one SD
    of the mean, 95% within two, 99.7% within three — so "mean 170, SD 7" instantly means "about 95% of
    people between 156 and 184".
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing, and defending the choice</h2>
  <p>
    Every measure is right somewhere and wrong somewhere, and exam marks live in the justification.
    <strong>Mean + SD</strong> for clean, roughly symmetric data with no extreme values: they use all the
    information and feed the normal-distribution machinery. <strong>Median + IQR</strong> for skewed data
    or data with outliers: incomes and house prices are right-skewed (a few very large values stretch the
    tail), so the median income and IQR describe the typical household far better than the mean. The
    <strong>mode</strong> for categorical data (modal shirt size is the one the shop must stock) and for
    bimodal data where a single "centre" would describe no one. Two quick diagnostics tell you which
    world you are in: compare the mean to the median (a big gap signals skew or outliers), and look at
    the histogram's tail. If the mean exceeds the median, the data are right-skewed and the median is
    the honest centre.
  </p>
  <p>
    Misleading summaries are also a communication problem. <strong>Simpson's paradox</strong>: aggregated
    numbers can reverse the story in every subgroup — the University of California, Berkeley (1973)
    appeared biased against women overall while most individual departments actually favoured them,
    because women applied disproportionately to the most competitive programmes; the aggregate answer
    and the disaggregated answer were opposite, and the disaggregated one was the truth.
    <strong>Misleading axes</strong>: a bar chart starting at 90 instead of 0 can make a 1% difference
    look like a landslide. The defence in both cases is the same: ask what the summary is quietly
    throwing away.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The mean–median relationship has an exact geometric meaning: the mean minimises the <em>sum of
    squared</em> deviations from any chosen point, while the median minimises the <em>sum of absolute</em>
    deviations — they are the optimal answers to different loss functions, which is why they respond to
    outliers differently (squared error punishes a huge deviation quadratically). The same logic scales
    up: variance is the expected squared deviation, and the reason statistics prefers squares over
    absolute values is largely that squares are analytically friendly — differentiable, additive across
    independent variables, and the seed of every confidence interval and significance test you will ever
    meet. Quartiles generalise to <strong>percentiles</strong> (the p-th value has p% of data below it —
    the 90th percentile of exam scores is the standard way to say "better than almost everyone"), and
    percentiles are what standardised testing reports because they are meaningful under <em>any</em>
    distribution, symmetric or not. One step further: a trimmed mean (delete the top and bottom 5%,
    then average) deliberately blends the mean's use of data with the median's resistance — sports
    judging uses exactly this compromise. The deep point is that "describing data" is really
    <em>modelling</em> it: every summary is a claim about which features of the data deserve to survive
    compression, and the statistician's judgement is about what gets lost.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bickel, Hammel & O'Connell, Science (1975) on Berkeley admissions; OpenStax, Introductory Statistics; Cambridge International AS & A Level Mathematics probability & statistics syllabus (measures of central tendency and dispersion).</p>
</TopicPage>
